# Recorded by hand from a reference runtime; see docs/reference-runtime.md.
# The model this refers to is NOT committed -- see docs/fixture-provenance.md
# for where to obtain it. No mandatory test reads this file.
#
# This one is here because every recording before it was of a model whose
# tokenizer merges by score. This one merges by rank from a table the file
# carries, cuts its text by the llama3 rule, and writes every byte as a
# printable stand-in -- a different road from the prompt to the tokens, and
# none of it was covered by a model that could actually generate.
#
# Tokenization came from:
#   llama-tokenize -m Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf
#     -p "The capital of France is" --ids
#     -> [128000, 791, 6864, 315, 9822, 374]
#   where 128000 is the beginning marker this vocabulary asks for.
#
# Greedy continuation came from llama-completion with --temp 0 --top-k 1
# --no-conversation, which printed " Paris." for the same prompt.
#
# The logits are the first three values of result_output from
# llama-eval-callback at the position after the prompt. The tolerance is 0.2
# because that is what these two implementations agree to on a four-bit
# quantization of a model this size; see the sizes recorded in
# docs/reference-runtime.md before reading anything into it.

runtime llama.cpp b1-717dad5
model Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf
prompt The capital of France is
tokens 128000 791 6864 315 9822 374
greedy_text \sParis.
logit 0 7.1842
logit 1 4.2274
logit 2 3.1886
tolerance 0.2
